  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">As trade rules, standards and regulatory regimes diverge, governments accelerate administrative rulemaking (new standards, procurement rules, compliance regimes) to protect domestic industries and align with preferred blocs.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d39">alliance_reliability_shi</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::trade_fragmentation" target="global_bond_market_rout::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Diverging rules and standards split pools of market makers, increase settlement and custody frictions across jurisdictions, and raise transaction costs and bid‑ask spreads, directly degrading tradability and liquidity in affected markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_market_rout</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::trade_fragmentation" target="mid_east_gulf_disruption::crude_export_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Growing divergence in trade rules, sanction lists and regulatory compliance across blocs increases paperwork, insurance costs and de‑risking by shippers and banks, causing reroutings, port denials and temporary halts that reduce net crude export volumes out of the Gulf.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d39">mid_east_gulf_disruption</data>
</edge>
